rt 2** (Lemma, lines 1304-1375, esp. lines 1340-1369): The exact characterization that **when a burst ends at a persistent state** ($m_t \\equiv 7 \\pmod{8}$), **the subsequent gap has length exactly 1**. This is a highly constrained bottleneck that your program's \"two-clock structure\" might relate to, but the paper formulates it as a cascade condition (persistence → gap length 1 deterministically).\n\n6. **The Phantom Family and 2-adic Repulsion** (Definition Phantom, Proposition Repulsion, lines 2164-2210): Phantom cycles are modular fixed points of block maps (signatures), and real integers in their residue classes experience **exponential 2-adic repulsion**: each block map decreases the 2-adic alignment by exactly $K$ bits (Prop. 2-adic Repulsion, lines 2194-2210). Your \"ghost cycles\" ($-1, -5, -17, -19/11$) inhabit similar terrain, but the paper's framework systematically catalogs the phantom families and bounds orbit shadows algebraically.\n\n7. **The Affine Census Theorem** (mentioned lines 2130-2143, details in Phantom sections): A deterministic bound on how many orbit points can \"shadow\" any given phantom family. This quantifies the expanding-family obstacle directly and controls the phantom-gain rate. Your program's \"depth-$g$ pool\" notion is conceptually similar but less formalized.\n\n8. **Information-Loss Language** (Remark Relation to Mixing, lines 2110-2122): The paper frames the Known-Zone Decay as **information loss** (bits determined by initial class shrink), which is distinct from mixing or equidistribution. This language clarifies the difference between algebraic rigidity (local information loss, exact) and probabilistic behavior (global mixing, requiring distributional assumptions).\n\n---\n\n### 6. KEY LINE NUMBERS FOR LOAD-BEARING STATEMENTS\n\n- **Lines 1100-1156**: Theorem (1/4 Persistent-Transition Law) with full proof\n- **Lines 1158-1174**: Corollary (Geometric Persistent Excursions, mean 4/3)\n- **Lines 1223-1246**: Theorem (Entr